      "text": "To find \\(\\sin \\frac{11 \\pi}{3}\\), we can use the periodicity of the sine function. The sine function has a period of \\(2\\pi\\), which means \\(\\sin(x) = \\sin(x + 2k\\pi)\\) for any integer \\(k\\). We will adjust \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) to an angle within one period of \\(2\\pi\\).\n\nFirst, we subtract \\(2\\pi\\) (which is \\(\\frac{6\\pi}{3}\\)) from \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = \\frac{11\\pi}{3} - \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nNext, we need to determine the value of \\(\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\). The angle \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is in the fourth quadrant, where the sine function is negative. The reference angle for \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n2\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{6\\pi}{3} - \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nThe sine of \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) is \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\), so:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = -\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
