      "text": "We start with the given mathematical problem:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{11 \\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nTo solve this, we will use the periodicity property of the sine function. The sine function is periodic with a period of \\(2\\pi\\), meaning:\n\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\sin (x + 2k\\pi) \\quad \\text{for any integer } k\n\\]\n\nWe need to convert \\(\\frac{11 \\pi}{3}\\) into an angle within the standard range \\([0, 2\\pi)\\). We do this by subtracting the appropriate multiple of \\(2\\pi\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11 \\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(3\\pi\\) is equivalent to \\(2\\pi\\) plus an additional \\(\\pi\\), we can simplify:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11 \\pi}{3} = \\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, \\(\\pi\\) is exactly half of \\(2\\pi\\), so:\n\n\\[\n\\pi = 1 \\cdot 2\\pi\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11 \\pi}{3} = \\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nWe now focus on the remaining angle:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(\\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\). The angle \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\) is in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. The reference angle for \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\pi - \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nThe sine of the reference angle \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) is \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). Since \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\) is in the second quadrant, the sine is positive:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{11 \\pi}{3} = \\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nPutting the final answer in the required format:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
