      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve for \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\), we need to reduce the angle \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) to an equivalent angle within the standard range of \\(0\\) to \\(2\\pi\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) in terms of multiples of \\(2\\pi\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\]\nSince \\(3\\pi\\) is equivalent to \\(\\pi\\) (because \\(3\\pi = 2\\pi + \\pi\\)), we can write:\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} = \\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\]\nNext, we use the fact that \\(\\sin(\\pi + \\theta) = -\\sin(\\theta)\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\left( \\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right) = -\\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\]\nNow, we need to find \\(\\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\). The angle \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\) is in the second quadrant, where the sine function is positive. The reference angle for \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\) is \\(\\pi - \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\nThus:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3} = -\\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
